<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Petrovaradin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1716">1716</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Peterwardein · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-turkish-war-1716-1718">Austro-Turkish War (1716–1718)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Petrovaradin was fought on 5 August 1716 at the fortress of Peterwardein, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, at the start of the Austro-Turkish War of 1716–1718. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> defeated a much larger Ottoman army under the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Silahdar Ali Pasha, who was killed. The victory opened the way to the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeswar</a> in 1716 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1717 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-petrovaradin.jpg" alt="Battle of Petrovaradin" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Philipp Rugendas the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg-philipp-rugendas-the-elder-1716-battle-of-petrovaradin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the summer of 1715 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> began to reclaim the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> had ceded to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1699, and easily retook the Venetian Kingdom of the Morea. Austria allied with Venice in April 1716 and demanded full Ottoman withdrawal and compensation to Venice. The Ottoman grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, confident that he could defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and even regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, declared war on 15 May 1716. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> with a 120,000-strong army that took nearly three months to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, where he consolidated a force of between 60,000 and 150,000, including 41,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">Sipahi</a> cavalry. They crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemun">Zemun</a> on 26 July and moved toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Sremski Karlovci</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> decided to meet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Petrovaradin, the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. He reached the fortress on 9 July, where 8,000 men, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, formed the garrison. On 2 August Field Marshal Janos Palffy's reconnaissance ran into over 10,000 Turkish cavalry, the Austrians lost 700 men and Field Marshal Siegfried Breuner was captured. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> demanded the fortress's surrender; Eugene answered, We will attack. His army crossed the Danube on 4 August by two pontoon bridges, under cover of an unusual summer snowstorm. It stood with one flank on the river and the other on the fortifications, with 64 battalions, 187 cavalry squadrons and 80 guns.</p><p>At seven in the morning on 5 August Eugene attacked. The left wing took the first Ottoman positions and a ten-gun battery, but in the centre the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> drove back Guido Starhemberg's troops into their entrenchments. When the Janissaries pushed on, they exposed both their flanks. Eugene sent cavalry against the left and Alexander of Wurttemberg's battalions against the right, while the fortress's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fired into the Turkish lines. The grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> was killed in a desperate charge with his bodyguard, along with 20,000 men. Imperial losses were 3,695 soldiers and 469 officers. The battle was over by 2 p.m., and Eugene reported the capture of 172 cannon and 156 banners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Pope Clement XI sent Eugene a consecrated sword. Within 20 days Eugene entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> with the help of Serbian irregulars and besieged Temesvar, in Ottoman hands since 1552; it surrendered on 16 October after 43 days. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> fell on 18 August 1717. A pilgrimage church was built at Tekije on the hill overlooking the battlefield, and a monument of 1902 by Hermann Bolle stands on Vezirac hill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Petrovaradin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>News of the <b>Battle of Petrovaradin</b> and the arrival of naval reinforcements forced the Ottomans to abandon their siege of Corfu, held by the Republic of Venice.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
